---

<PolicyPage
  title="Care Instructions"
  eyebrow="Making it last"
  description="How to wash, dry, block and store crocheted pieces and the yarn they are made from, without felting anything."
  intro="Crochet outlives almost everything else in a wardrobe if it is treated gently. Most of that is one rule — do not shock it with heat or agitation."
  updated="2026-08-28"
>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Read the band first.</strong> Every yarn I sell keeps its own label, and what the
      manufacturer says beats anything on this page. Superwash merino and pure alpaca want opposite
      things.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Washing</h2>

  <p>
    Hand wash in cool water — lukewarm at most, and the same temperature start to finish. It is not
    heat on its own that felts wool, it is the change in temperature combined with movement, so a
    cold rinse after a warm wash is exactly what to avoid.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Fill a basin with cool water and a little wool wash or mild shampoo.</li>
    <li>Submerge the piece and press it gently. Do not rub, wring or swirl.</li>
    <li>Leave it fifteen minutes.</li>
    <li>Rinse in water of the same temperature until it runs clear.</li>
    <li>Press the water out against the side of the basin — never twist.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Roll the piece in a dry towel and press to take the last of the water out. Then lay it flat, in
    shape, out of direct sun. A wet crocheted piece hung on a line will stretch under its own weight
    and stay stretched.
  </p>

  <h3>The machine</h3>

  <p>
    Only if the band says superwash, and only then in a mesh bag on a wool cycle at 30°C or below,
    with the spin as low as it goes. Never a tumble dryer. Cotton and acrylic pieces tolerate a
    gentle machine wash better than wool does, but they still lose their shape in a hot dryer.
  </p>

  <h2>Blocking</h2>

  <p>
    Blocking is what turns a slightly wonky square into a flat one, and it is the difference between
    a project that looks homemade and one that looks handmade. It is worth doing once, after
    finishing, and again after each wash if the piece needs it.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Wet blocking</strong> — soak, squeeze out, pin to shape on a towel or a foam mat, and
      leave until bone dry. Best for wool.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Steam blocking</strong> — pin to shape dry, then hover a steam iron just above the
      surface without touching it. Fast, and good for cotton. Never on acrylic.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Acrylic</strong> — heat permanently "kills" the fibre and it will not come back. If you
      want a crisp edge on an acrylic piece that is how to get it, but there is no undoing it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Storing</h2>

  <p>
    Fold, do not hang. A hanger puts the entire weight of a garment on two points of a fabric built
    to stretch, and shoulders never recover.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Somewhere dry and dark — sunlight fades dyed yarn faster than washing does.</li>
    <li>Loosely, with room to breathe. A vacuum bag crushes the stitch pattern flat.</li>
    <li>
      Cedar or lavender near wool, not mothballs. Moths go for natural fibres and ignore acrylic
      entirely.
    </li>
    <li>Clean before storing for a season. Moths are drawn to the traces we leave in fabric.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Pilling and snags</h2>

  <p>
    Pills are normal on any yarn that has a halo, and they are not a fault — they are short fibres
    working loose. A fabric shaver or a sweater comb takes them off. Do not pull them; that drags the
    fibres they are attached to.
  </p>

  <p>
    A snagged loop is not a hole. Do not cut it. Use a crochet hook or a needle to work the pulled
    strand back through to the wrong side and ease the stitches either side of it back into shape.
  </p>

  <h2>Looking after your tools</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Wipe wooden and bamboo hooks dry, and keep them out of water — they can warp and splinter.</li>
    <li>A little wax or oil on a wooden hook once a year keeps the shaft smooth.</li>
    <li>Store hooks in a roll or a case. Loose in a drawer is how points get chipped.</li>
    <li>Keep stitch markers and scissors somewhere they cannot snag your yarn.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When in doubt</h2>

  <p>
    Test on something that does not matter — a swatch, a tail, the inside of a seam. And if you are
    unsure what a particular piece will take, <a href={`mailto:${SHOP_EMAIL}`}>ask me</a>. I know
    what went into it, and I would much rather answer than hear that something felted.
  </p>
</PolicyPage>
